     ============

         Long option          Equivalent to short option
         ArenaCombat          -ac
         AutoOpenDoors        -aod
         AutoPickupGold       -apg
         AutoPickupTorch      -apt
         AutoSearchWalls      -asw
         AutoTarget           -at
         Casual               -c
         DungeonScaling       -eds
         Epic                 -e
         FullPartyCombat      -fcd
         Heroic               -h
         Music                -mu
         NoArenaComabe        -nac
         NoAutoOpenDoors      -naod
         NoAutoPickupgold     -napg
         NoAutoPickuptorch    -napt
         NoAutoSearchWalls    -nasw
         NoAutoTarget         -nat
         NoDungeonScaling     -nds
         NoFullPartyCombat    -rcd
         NoMusic              -nmu
         Normal               -n
         ShowStats            -s
         DelayHit             -dh
         DelayTraps           -dt
         DelayRanged          -dr
         Depth                -d
         MonsterCount         -mc
         Width                -w
         Height               -h
         downgradedepth depth Permenently change the maximum depth in a
                              saved game
         downgradearena       Permenently turn off the arena comabe mode in
                              a saved game.
         FKeyGear             Unshifted F keys select gear
         FKeyMacros           Unshifted F keys select macros
         FKeySpells           Unshifted F keys select spells
         FKeyWeapons          Unshifted F keys select weapons
         Fn=macro             Assigne Fn macro (Ex. F1=Rs)
         GameThread           The game runs on its own thread; the window
                              always stays responsive.  The default
         NoGameThread         The game and the window share one thread, as
                              in older versions
         IntegerScaling       Scale the game screen by whole numbers only
                              (2x, 3x, ...), with black borders
         NoIntegerScaling     Fill the window, blending only the edges
                              between pixels.  The default
         MusicVolume=percent  Volume of the background music, 0 to 100 in
                              steps of 10.  The default is 100
         MuteWhenUnfocused=On Mute all audio while the game's window is
                              out of focus (see Music).  Off keeps it
                              playing.  The default is On
         WorldMap=style       What M shows on the upland: Off, Color or
                              Art (see World Map).  The default is Art
         ColorPalette=name    The 16 colours of text, frames and maps:
                              X11 or DOS (see Color Palette).  The
                              default is X11


       note: The game option switches (-d, -h, -e, -n, -c, -at, -nat,
     -apg, -napg, -apt, -napt, -aod, -naod, -asw, -nasw, -ac, -nac, -eds,
     -nds, -fcd, -rcd, -mc, -dr, -dh, -dt) and their long options are
     deprecated.  They only set the starting values of the New Game
     Options screen.  Each character keeps its own options in its save
     file, so these switches have no effect on a game that is loaded.
     See "Game Options".

       note: Using -downgradedepth can only make dungeons shallower to a
     minimum depth of 2.  You cannot specify a depth that is greater than
     what the save file is currently set to.

       note: Using -downgradearena allows you to permenently turn off the
     arena combat mode in a saved game.  Once it is turned off you cannot
     turn it back on.

       WARNING!  There is a downside to decreasing the dungeon depth,
     turning off arena combat mode or decreasing the monster spawn rate in
     that with fewer monsters to fight you will gain levels at a slower
     rate and also with fewer battles means fewer drops and fewer chances
     to have that really special piece of gear drop.

     unicorn.cfg
     ===========
       The game will look for and load options from the file 'unicorn.cfg' 
     All the command line options except the downgrade options may be
     specified in this file.  This way if you have common options you like
     to use, you can put them in this file and then you will no longer need
     to specify them on the command line.  Game options in this file only
     set the starting values of the New Game Options screen.

     Character config file
     =====================
       New to version 6.3 is the ability to load character specific config
     files. Character config files are checked after the unicorn.cfg and
     command line arguments are parsed.  As such the character specific
     config options override all others.  To create a character specific
     config file, name it after your character with a .cfg extension.  For
     example, If you character is named Xun, then the character specific
     config file would be named Xun.cfg.  The main use for character
     specific configs is how the function keys are used.  For example on a
     warrior you may prefer the function keys access the gear manager while
     on a mage you would want them to access your spells.  Or you may want
     to define different macros depending on the character.  Game options
     are the exception: a loaded character always uses the options stored
     in its save file.

     Loading a Saved Game
     ====================
       The title screen offers New Game and Load Game.  Use 8 and 2 (or
     the arrow keys) to choose one and ENTER to select it.  Load Game
     lists the saved games (.sav files) in the game's folder, most
     recently saved first, with each character's class, level, game
     mode and time played.  A file the game cannot load is shown as
     Unreadable.  Use 8 and 2 to select a game, 9 and 3 (or
     PgUp and PgDn) to move a page at a time, ENTER to load it and ESC to
     return to the title screen.  A game loaded this way reads its
     character config file too, except for the screen size, which is
     fixed once the window is open.

       A saved game can also be loaded by giving the name of the save file
     (no extension) on the command line. ex.  UNICORN JUDY

     Game Options
     ============
       Before a new character is created, the New Game Options screen lets
     you choose how the game will play.  Use 8 and 2 (or the arrow keys)
     to select an option, 4 and 6 to change it, TAB to move to the next
     group (rules, preferences, gameplay changes), and ENTER when done.
     TAB returns to the option last selected in a group.  Options with
     grey names cannot be changed, but can still be selected to read
     about them.  The screen starts with the values from unicorn.cfg and
     the command line.  A switch shows On (or Strict) in blue and Off in
     grey.  A number or mode shows yellow at the game's own default and
     blue when changed.  Long lists scroll, with a bar beside them.

       Character rules are fixed once the character is created:

         Game Mode            Normal, Heroic, Epic or Casual (see below)
         Permadeath           Death is final (see below)
         Dungeon Depth        Levels in each dungeon, 2 to 10
         Dungeon Scaling      Monsters in dungeons scale to your level
         Monster Count        Spawn size for monsters or groups, 10 to 60
         Arena Combat         On: multi-monster arena combat
                              Off: only bosses
         Full Party Combat    Monsters and party members take full damage

       Gameplay changes are listed to the right of the rules.  Each one
     changes how the original game plays, most often to fix a bug.  Like
     the rules, they are chosen on the New Game Options screen and are
     fixed once the character is created.  A new character starts with
     every change On, and Castle Treasure at Strict; Off plays as the
     original game.  A game saved before a change existed keeps it Off.
     The list scrolls, and "more" marks changes above or below what
     fits on the screen.

         World Seed           A new world picks from 2 billion dungeon
                              layouts.  Off, from only 32,767
         New Game Dungeon     A new character's first visit to the
                              Temple of the Forest builds its levels.
                              Off, if it is the first dungeon entered,
                              its levels are empty and you stand in a
                              corner of nothing
         Quest State          The save remembers which dungeons you
                              have entered, so after a load the
                              compass still lists only the dungeons
                              you have entered or heard of.  Off, a
                              loaded game's compass lists every
                              dungeon
         Compass              The compass points the right way to and
                              from the easternmost regions.  Off, it
                              places them at the west end of the next
                              row of regions, and can point the wrong
                              way
         Lost Entrance        One dungeon is entered from its lost entrance
                              at the end of a canyon, and the compass
                              points there.  Off, it cannot be entered, the
                              compass points at the mountains to the north,
                              and the Completionist trophy leaves it out
         Town Completion      Entering a town, on foot or by ship, marks
                              it complete in the Quest Log, so the
                              Completionist trophy can be won.  Off,
                              towns are never complete and the trophy
                              cannot be won
         Town Trophy          Completionist counts only the dungeons,
                              and visiting every town earns a trophy of
                              its own, Sightseer (towns are marked by
                              Town Completion).  Off, Completionist
                              wants the towns too
         Free Upland Keys     On the upland, keys that only show
                              something or do nothing pass no time:
                              help, the record, options, icons, ~,
                              the travel log (T), Q's question, X, keys
                              with no use, a step into mountains or
                              water, and an inventory, item use or
                              gear set left unchanged.  A blocked step
                              is not counted as a step taken.  Off,
                              each one passes an hour or more, eats
                              food and can bring an encounter
         Upland Quit          Q in the upland asks before ending the
                              character, as it does in towns and
                              dungeons.  Off, it quits at once
         Explored Land        A game saved on the upland with S or the
                              TAB menu keeps the land you explored in
                              the region you are in.  Off, loading it
                              forgets what you explored there since you
                              last entered the region
         Hunting Skill        Hunting on the upland (h, or s) finds
                              food more often as your Scouting grows,
                              the skill it trains.  Off, it goes by
                              your Mace skill
         Ship Purchase        A ship bought from a travel agency is
                              yours at once, docked in that harbor, so
                              a second is refused.  Off, it counts as
                              yours only once you board it and step off,
                              and each purchase before then costs 2500
         Ship Improvements    Your ship stays in a town's harbour and
                              is there each time you return.  Sailing
                              onto a town with a harbour (Radon,
                              Marduk) docks you in it, and sailing out
                              of the harbour brings you out on the
                              water beside the town.  Walking onto your
                              ship boards it, and moving twice towards
                              land you can walk on steps ashore: on the
                              coast, or at a town without a harbour,
                              the ship waits on the water; in a harbour
                              it goes back to the dock.  A ship never
                              docks on a town.  Off, a ship left in a
                              town appears on the overworld at sea or
                              on a mountain, a ship docked on a town's
                              square hides the town, and boarding and
                              leaving a ship take E
         Travel Saves         A save keeps a relic's travel destinations.
                              Off, loading a game saved with a destination
                              programmed loses the destinations and the
                              messages and lore you have read, and in a
                              town or dungeon puts you at a destination's
                              coordinates
         Sleep Traps          A sleep trap puts you to sleep.  Off, it
                              never springs
         Words of Power       Rising by a word of power from the first
                              level of a dungeon takes you out of it,
                              as the stairs do, and words that sink you,
                              to the next level or to the bottom, do
                              nothing in a town.  Off, rising from a
                              first level freezes the game, and sinking
                              in a town drops you onto a level of the
                              last dungeon you were in, or freezes the
                              game if there is none
         Final Dungeon Exit   Leaving the final dungeon ends its rules,
                              and its entrance shows again.  Off, every
                              dungeon after it is built as the final
                              dungeon, until the game is won
         Final Boss Spawns    The final boss waits on the fifth level of
                              the final dungeon.  Off, it never appears,
                              so the game cannot be won
         Den of Horror Guards Guards protect a relic on the bottom level
                              of the Den of Horror.  Off, they never
                              appear
         Castle Treasure      The final boss's castle, the final dungeon's
                              fifth level, has one treasure of its own on
                              its floor at a time.  Strict: none appears
                              while you own one; with Possessions Off one
                              used in crafting still counts as yours.  A
                              new character starts at Strict.  Off, every
                              visit while the final boss lives adds one
         Sun Stone            Returning to a dungeon by Sun Stone leaves
                              its levels as they were.  Off, a return to
                              level 2 or deeper saves the town you came
                              from over the level above
         Sun Stone Final Dungeon
                              A Sun Stone used in the final dungeon brings
                              you back to it.  Off, the return reads a
                              place past the end of the game's list, and
                              leaving the dungeon again can crash the game
         Sun Stone Fixes      A Sun Stone records only a dungeon level,
                              not the upland or an arena fight, and
                              keeps that level as you left it.  It
                              returns you to the dungeon it holds,
                              rebuilding it if you went elsewhere,
                              and does nothing if it holds no level.
                              Off, a return can land in the last
                              dungeon you visited, monsters and items
                              you dealt with are back, and an empty
                              stone sends you to the Northern Realm
         Elemental Weapon Damage
                              A weapon whose physical and elemental
                              damage are equal deals that damage.  Off,
                              it deals none
         Ranged Damage        An arrow or thrown weapon rolls for a
                              critical hit once, and its damage counts
                              once in your statistics.  Off, it can
                              critical twice, for four times the damage,
                              and every hit is counted twice
         Thrown Weapons       Thrown rocks and stars that land on an
                              item do nothing.  Off, the game crashes
         Flask of Oil         An arrow that hits a Flask of Oil on the
                              floor breaks it, and it bursts into
                              flame, burning the creatures beside it.
                              Off, the arrow does nothing to it
         Poison Kills         A creature that poison kills leaves the
                              map, and drops its loot, where it died.
                              Off, it stays standing there, dead, and
                              the ground under you is changed instead
         Summon Combat        A monster fighting your summoned creature
                              hits with its own attack against the
                              creature's defense, arena monsters go for
                              it and cannot walk over it, and it finds
                              its way back to you.  Off, a monster aims
                              at the wrong defense and hits with the
                              creature's own attack, and the creature
                              can lose its way
         Party Joining        A creature that joins your party gets its
                              party attack, defense and mana.  Off, it
                              keeps its own, and the party values go to
                              another slot, often past the party and
                              into your own data
         Teleport Spell       Moves you to a random spot on a dungeon
                              level, and fizzles in towns, the upland,
                              the arena and the final boss's realm.  Off,
                              it spends mana and does nothing
         Blink Only Blinks    Blink only moves you.  Off, it also casts
                              Protection
         Blink Landing        Blink leaves you where it lands.  Off, a
                              blink near the right or bottom edge of a
                              level can put you in a wall
         Spell Targets        Blind, sleep and drain spells, and spells
                              your ring reflects, affect the creature
                              they hit.  Off, outside the arena they
                              miss it and change some other record
         Blast Spells         Nuke, Nova, Word of God, Blizzard,
                              Conflagration and Electrical Nova spare
                              anti-magic creatures and cost alignment
                              only when they hit a friend.  Off, they
                              hurt most anti-magic creatures, cost
                              alignment for almost any creature, and
                              scramble the abilities of those hit
         Spell Kills          A creature killed by a blast, Super Nova,
                              Armageddon, a drain or a reflected spell
                              counts as a kill, can drop loot, and its
                              exp is shared as for any kill.  Off, you
                              only get its exp, and killing the final boss
                              this way does not win the game
         Scroll of Charging   Restores the chosen item's charges.  Off,
                              the game freezes
         Mind Shield          A Potion or Scroll of Mind Shield wears
                              off like the other resistances.  Off, it
                              lasts forever
         Arena Assassins      An Assassin keeps its level in arena
                              combat.  Off, every turn in the arena
                              starts an Assassin over at level 1
         Arena Monk Fear      Monks cannot be frightened in the arena
                              from level 11, as in dungeons.  Off, not
                              until level 14
         Arena Timers         Cold, electric and arcane resistance,
                              blindness, light and polymorph wear off
                              during arena fights, as in dungeons.
                              Off, they pause until the fight ends
         Arena Decorations    Trees, rocks and other scenery in an
                              upland arena land only on open ground.
                              Off, scenery can land on a monster, which
                              then cannot be fought, or on a party
                              member, which sits out the fight
         Arena Exit           ENTER or ESC leaves an arena fight once
                              every monster is dead.  Off, the only way
                              out is to walk off the arena's edge
         Arena Held Keys      Keys held down or typed ahead as an
                              arena fight starts or ends are dropped.
                              Off, the game also waits for one more
                              key and drops it: before a fight the
                              map you were on stays, and after one
                              the map shows without you
         Safe Arena Loot      Items, gold and torches still lying on
                              the arena's floor when a dungeon fight
                              ends are laid on the free ground nearest
                              you.  Off, they are lost with the arena.
                              Upland fights leave theirs either way
         Rarity Fix           How picking up an item counts it
                              towards your finds (the Eye for
                              Treasure, Treasure Hunter and Lucky!
                              trophies and the statistics).  Off,
                              getting it with g counts it as its card
                              names it, while packing it with G counts
                              some items as commoner than that and the
                              commonest Ultra Rare ones not at all.
                              Band A counts both as g does, Band B
                              both as G does, and Band C as G does but
                              with those Ultra Rare ones counted.  A
                              new character starts at Band A
         Unified Pickup       Packing an item from the floor with G
                              does all that getting it with g does: a
                              trading card joins your collection, a
                              dungeon's treasure on its last level
                              completes the quest and is identified, a
                              recipe item gets its recipe, and a
                              mutable item's level is set as g sets
                              it, so the Tab menu's Pick Up packs every
                              item unless the pack is full.  Off, only
                              g does those
         Floor Items          Putting an item from the floor straight
                              into your pack frees its place in the
                              level's list of floor items, as does
                              an arrow breaking one, or leaving an
                              item on an arena's floor or in the final
                              boss's realm, which is laid out afresh
                              at each visit, and when that list is
                              full a slain monster drops nothing
                              instead of an item.  Off, those items
                              keep their place, and a full list leaves
                              a stray tile where the item should have
                              been
         Lore Books           Putting a lore book straight into your
                              pack does nothing; read it with g as
                              usual.  Off, that can copy an item from
                              your Portable Hole into your pack and
                              remove the book until you return to the
                              level
         Pack Weight          The item in the last pack slot counts
                              toward the weight you carry.  Off, it
                              weighs nothing
         Possessions          An item you used up, threw or sold no
                              longer counts as yours when treasure is
                              chosen.  Off, until you save and reload,
                              a unique item you no longer have will not
                              drop again, and items that need another
                              item still drop after it is gone
         Item Deck            The Item Deck never gives the broken "EOL"
                              item.  Off, it occasionally does
         Tripped Card         The Deck of Many Things' Tripped card
                              takes your own class's hit die.  Off, it
                              takes the next class's, and a Shaman's is
                              read from outside the game's data
         Manual of Bodily Health
                              The Manual of Bodily Health only adds to
                              your hit points.  Off, it also uses the
                              Crown of Might, saying the Triad is
                              incomplete or, with the Triad on, giving
                              the crown's powers for free
         Shaman Legendaries   Ayres' Mythical items found by a Shaman
                              get the Cleric's bonuses, and the weapon
                              is a mace.  Off, the armour has no
                              bonuses and the weapon is an edged one
                              no one can train for
         Last Bite            Eating the last bite of a food with e
                              counts as a meal, as it does with u.
                              Off, it is not counted
         Meat and Potatoes    The Meat and Potatoes trophy counts the
                              meat you eat, and meat counts once as a
                              meal.  Off, meat counts twice as a meal
                              and 100 potatoes alone earn the trophy
         Village Shops        Every village's four merchants trade:
                              weapons and armour of levels 1 and 2,
                              supplies, and an inn.  Off, they never
                              open
         Shop Boots and Wands Armor shops sell cloth, leather and copper
                              boots, and magic shops sell the Wands of
                              Pine, Ceder and Redwood.  Off, no shop
                              sells boots or wands
         Shop Dagger          Weapon shops list the Dagger once, as
                              version 5 did.  Off, they list it twice,
                              as 6.x and 7.0 did: a second copy comes
                              from a placeholder at the head of the
                              item table, the same dagger but of no
                              rarity, so its name is yellow
         Platinum Dragon Mount
                              A Platinum Dragon bought at Nichorville's
                              stable carries you and flies, like the
                              other dragons.  Off, it joins your party
                              but is no mount
         Temple Uncurse       Leaving a temple's Remove Curse without
                              choosing an item returns to its menu.
                              Off, the game crashes
         Library Text         A town library shows its messages in plain
                              text.  Off, they come out enciphered
         Lore Search          A library or computer search shows and
                              teaches the message that matched.  Off,
                              it gives the message after it
         House Buying         Owning a house never stops you buying
                              another.  Off, owning Goldville as your
                              first house, Radon as your second,
                              Nichorville as your third or Talfharin as
                              your fourth stops every later purchase
         House Storage        A house's storage shows all of its 500
                              slots, on 13 pages, and you cannot leave
                              a full house with an item In Air.  Off,
                              the last 20 slots never show, and an item
                              In Air is lost when you leave a full house
         No Negative Amounts  A negative amount typed at a bank, a house
                              sale or the dice table counts as 0, which
                              at the dice table means leaving.  Off, it
                              adds gold

       Preferences can be changed at any time by pressing O in the upland,
     in towns and in dungeons:

         Music Volume         Volume of the background music, 0% to 100%
         Mute When Unfocused  Mute all audio while the game's window is
                              out of focus.  With the focus back, the
                              music plays at its volume again
         Color Palette        The 16 colours the game draws its text,
                              frames and maps in: X11, the colours the
                              Windows game has always drawn, or DOS,
                              the CGA, EGA and VGA colours of the DOS
                              release, whose reds, greens and blues
                              are softer.  The tile art keeps its own
                              colours either way
         Auto-Target          Target the nearest monster automatically
         Auto-Open Doors      Doors open automatically
         Auto-Search Walls    Search walls automatically
         Auto-Pickup Gold     Pick up gold automatically
         Auto-Pickup Torches  Pick up torches automatically
         Improved Interface   Choose in shops, the library, the
                              inventory, the party screen and a new
                              character's sex, race and class with a
                              cursor, as on the options screen, answer
                              their questions from a menu, see item
                              names in the colours of their rarity (see
                              Item Merchants), see the names of towns
                              and dungeons when you look around (v),
                              read scrolls in the middle of the screen,
                              see how far you are through your level on
                              a yellow bar under Level, and open a menu
                              of the commands you can use where you
                              stand with Tab (see Tab Menu).  Off, the
                              game looks and asks as it first did
         Easy Spells          Spells in the Tab menu lists the spells
                              you know to pick from, instead of asking
                              you to type one (see Tab Menu).  It needs
                              Improved Interface, and is greyed out
                              while that is Off
         Easy Words           Say a Word of Power in the Tab menu lists
                              the words you have read of to pick from,
                              a word you type is filled in once its
                              first letters fit only one of them, and
                              the message log says when a word fails
                              (see Words of Power), and so does a relic
                              that answers to words.  It needs
                              Improved Interface, and is greyed out
                              while that is Off.  Off by default
         Improved Status      Show the status indicators under your
                              stats (see Status Indicators) as grey
                              letters (Off), coloured letters (Color),
                              or the icons themselves at half size
                              (Icons).  Icons also gives the row of
                              icons along the bottom of the screen to
                              the map, which shows one more row.  The
                              small map moves down to the edge of the
                              screen, and the message log grows into
                              its place, with the compass and item
                              names on its last line; the Tab menu
                              covers that line too
         Floor Item Names     Show the name of the item you stand on in the
                              bottom right, or under the message log (see
                              Improved Status), in its item colour (see
                              Item Colors)
         Improved Items       Names show in the colour of the rarity
                              their item card names (see Item Colors),
                              except a quest item's, in cyan, and,
                              once identified, a unique item's, in
                              brown, a legendary one's, in light red,
                              or red if unique too, and legacy gear's,
                              in yellow, and the card says Unique,
                              Legendary or Unique Legendary in place
                              of the rarity.  An item you have not
                              identified shows in its rarity's colour
                              instead of cyan, with a * before its
                              name on the floor, in your inventory and
                              pack, a house and the portable hole, and
                              a common item a monster drops is
                              identified, as its first pickup would
                              identify it anyway (see Auto-Identify).
                              Off, names follow older colour bands,
                              and an unidentified item is cyan, and
                              yellow on the floor
         Improved Combat Logs Each line of the message log says who hit
                              whom and for how much, and how a fight
                              ends: "The Goblin hits you for 3", "You
                              miss the Goblin", "The Goblin dies! (+9
                              exp)".  Spells name themselves, as do
                              your bow, wand or what you throw, and
                              traps, effects that wear off, and the
                              levels, stats and skills you gain say
                              what changed and by how much.  A shot
                              that destroys an item on the floor names
                              it.  The log fills the whole width of its
                              box.  Off, the log keeps the game's first
                              lines, such as "OUCH! for 3" and "Exp=9"
         Enhanced Sprites     Show the tile under creatures and items
         Unique Class Sprites Show your class as its own figure where the
                              game has one: a Cavalier in mail with a
                              lance, a Cleric or Shaman robed in white,
                              and a Mage, Enchanter, Druid or Hierophant
                              robed in blue.  Other classes keep the
                              standard figure
         Show Mount           On the overworld, show the mount you ride
                              in place of your own figure: the first
                              flying mount in your party when you fly,
                              otherwise the first mount
         Seamless World Map   The overworld is one map: walking off a
                              region's edge goes on into the next with
                              no change of screen, and you see and
                              explore across the edge.  The small map
                              follows you, centred where the world
                              allows (On), or shows the region you are
                              in (Region).  Off, each region is its own
                              screen, as before
         Follow Camera        The map view follows you, on the overworld
                              and in towns and dungeons: you walk freely
                              in the middle fifth of it, and beyond that
                              the view moves with you a square at a time
                              instead of jumping, stopping at the map's
                              edges.  Arriving somewhere (a level, a
                              town, the overworld, stairs, a teleport or
                              a Tardis) centres the view on you, as far
                              as the edges allow.  The arena's view stays
                              as it is
         World Map            What M shows on the upland (see World
                              Map): nothing (Off), the world in the
                              small map's colours (Color), or with each
                              tile's own art (Art)
         Gothic Scrolls       Write scrolls, lore and the library in
                              Gothic instead of the hand-drawn scroll
                              letters.  Either way each line is centred,
                              and text too long for the scroll moves
                              with Up/Down and PgUp/PgDn
         Ranged Delay (ms)    Ranged attack flight delay
         Hit Delay (ms)       Successful hit effect delay
         Trap Delay (ms)      Trap effects delay

       Music Volume is heard at once as it changes, and is saved in
     unicorn.cfg as MusicVolume, so it applies to every character.  Mute
     When Unfocused, World Map and Color Palette are kept there too, as
     MuteWhenUnfocused, WorldMap and ColorPalette.  All the other options
     are saved with the character, so every saved game keeps its own
     options.  A game saved by an older version keeps its mode, depth,
     monster count and arena setting, and takes the other options from
     unicorn.cfg and the command line the first time it is loaded.

     Modes of Play
     =============
       Quest now suports 4 modes of play.  They are as follows:

       Normal - This is the standard mode.  Players qualify for scores as
     well as rebirth.  Monsters are at the normal level.  Permadeath is
     on unless it is turned off on the New Game Options screen.

       Heroic - This is meant to give more of a challenge.  This mode also
     qualifies for scoring and rebirth.  Monsters are double the strength
     of those in normal mode.  When a score is recorded in the heroic mode
     the exp field will be shown in red to show that the score was obtained
     in heroic mode.  Like normal, permadeath can be turned off.

       Epic   - This is an even tougher game.  This mode qualifies for
     scoring and rebirth.  Monsters in this mode are 4 times stronger than
     those in normal mode.  When a score is recorded in the epic mode the
     exp field will be shown in purple to show that the score was obtained
     in epic mode.  Like normal, permadeath can be turned off.

       Casual - This is meant for a more casual game.  In this mode
     permadeath is not active.  If you die you can reload from your last
     save.  As a consequence of playing in this mode no scores will be
     recorded and you will not qualify for the Ritual of Rebirth.

       The mode is chosen on the New Game Options screen when the character
     is created, and is recorded in the character data.

       Permadeath is chosen on the same screen.  With it, a character that
     dies or quits is lost for good, since its save file is deleted.
     Without it you can reload from your last save, and the character
     still qualifies for scores and rebirth.  Casual mode never has
     permadeath, and a character that becomes Casual loses it and keeps
     its last save.  In the Hall of Legends and the Hall of Remembrance,
     the PD column shows Y for a character with permadeath, N without.

       When a character dies or quits, its score is recorded and the game
     returns to the title screen to start a new character.  The game only
     closes after an exit with save.

       S saves the game and play continues.  With permadeath the save is
     held as a backup while the character plays, and is deleted when it
     dies or quits, so the character can only be loaded again after an
     exit with save (X).

     Upland Commands
     ===============
         8 - Move up
         2 - Move down
         4 - Move left
         6 - Move Right
         7 - Move up and left
         9 - Move up and right
         1 - Move down and left
         3 - Move down and right
         i - Show inventory
         Q - Quit, no save
         X - Exit with save
         S - Save and continue
         > - Enter dungeon or town
         e - Eat
         h - Hunt for food
         E - Enter/Exit a ship
         R - Show player record
         M - World map
         O - Options
         { - Save weapon set
         [ - Recall weapon set
         } - Save gear set
         ] - Recall gear set
         ? - List commands

     Dungeon Commands
     ================
         8 - Move up
         2 - Move down
         4 - Move left
         6 - Move Right
         7 - Move up and left
         9 - Move up and right
         1 - Move down and left
         3 - Move down and right
         5 - Run
         Q - Quit, no save.  note: This will abandon the character
         X - Exit with save
         > - Down level
         < - Up level
         s - Search
         o - Open a door
         c - Close a door
         J - Jam a door closed
         i - Show inventory
         g - Get an item
         q - Show quest log
         u - Use an item
         E - Enter/Exit a ship
         f - Fire weapon in right hand
         m - Cast a magic spell
         n - Take a Nap
         t - Talk to an adjacent character
         T - Throw an item
         j - Join another member to your party
         p - Party commands
         l - Light a torch
         L - Show message log
         w - Say a Word of Power
         b - Buy an item (Must be standing near a merchant)
         S - Save and continue
         R - Show player record
         O - Options
         H - Show icons
         x - Transcribe spell
         - - Throw left hand
         = - Throw right hand
         { - Save weapon set
         [ - Recall weapon set
         } - Save gear set
         ] - Recall gear set
         ? - Show commands

     Combat Commands
     ===============
         8 - Move up
         2 - Move down
         4 - Move left
         6 - Move Right
         7 - Move up and left
         9 - Move up and right
         1 - Move down and left
         3 - Move down and right
         i - Show inventory
         g - Get an item
         u - Use an item
         f - Fire weapon in right hand
         m - Cast a magic spell
         T - Throw an item
         p - Party commands
         V - Turn sound on/off
         R - Show player record
         H - Show icons
         - - Throw left hand
         = - Throw right hand
         { - Save weapon set
         [ - Recall weapon set
         } - Save gear set
         ] - Recall gear set
         ? - Show commands

     Keys
     ====
         Typing follows the Windows keyboard layout, with Shift and Caps
         Lock working as usual.  Only plain ASCII characters reach the
         game; accented letters and other symbols are ignored.

         The keypad moves the same with or without NumLock: the arrows,
         Home, End, PgUp and PgDn give 8, 2, 4, 6, 7, 1, 9 and 3, and the
         keypad's centre key gives 5.  The arrow, Home, End, PgUp and
         PgDn keys beside the keypad do the same.  F1 .. F12 work alone,
         with Shift and with Ctrl (Ctrl wins when both are held), and Alt
         with the number row stands in for Alt with the F keys; see
         "F-key usage".

         These keys work on every screen:

         Alt+Enter - Borderless Window Toggle
         Alt+F4    - Force Close
         Ctrl+P    - Screenshot
         Ctrl+M    - Music Toggle

         Alt+F4 does what the window's close button does.  Nothing is
         saved, and a character that is not Casual is lost, since its
         save file is deleted when it is loaded.  Use X to exit with
         save instead.

         Alt on its own and F10 do not open the window menu, so F10 is an
         F key like the others.  Alt+Space still opens it.

     Screenshots
     ===========
         Ctrl+P - Take a screenshot.
                  Writes to screenshotNNNN.bmp in the game directory.

     Window size
     ===========
         The window can be resized or maximized.  The game screen grows
         to fill the window while keeping its shape, and it is centred
         with black borders.  At whole steps (2x, 3x, ...) every pixel
         stays sharp; in between, only the edges of pixels are blended
         so they all stay the same size.  With IntegerScaling the game
         screen only grows by whole steps, so every pixel stays sharp.
         The window cannot be made smaller than the game screen set by
         -w and -h.  The window opens at the whole step that best matches
         the display scaling.

     Fullscreen
     ==========
         Alt+Enter - Switch between the window and fullscreen.  Works on
                     every screen.

         Fullscreen covers the whole monitor the window is on, with no
         frame or title bar, and the game screen is scaled to it the same
         way as in the window.  Alt+Enter again puts the window back as
         it was.  Fullscreen is a borderless window rather than a change
         of display mode, so Alt+Tab switches to other programs at once.

     Music
     =====
         The game plays the background music of
         the MS-DOS release.  The intro theme plays from the title
         screen until play begins.  After that the upland theme plays
         in the wilderness, the town theme in towns and the dungeon
         theme in dungeons, each repeating until you move on.  The
         songs are MIDI files in the 'music' folder beside the game.
         Music is on by default; NoMusic or -nmu starts with it off.
         Ctrl+M - Turn the music off or back on.  Works on every
                  screen.
         The Music Volume preference (O) sets how loud it plays, from
         0 to 100 percent in steps of 10.  It is the game's slider in
         the Windows volume mixer, and is saved in unicorn.cfg.
         With the Mute When Unfocused preference (O), on by default,
         the game falls silent while its window is out of focus, and
         its slider in the volume mixer reads 0.  Once you return to
         the window, the music plays at its volume again.  It is
         saved in unicorn.cfg as MuteWhenUnfocused.

     Maps
     ====
         The upland and the towns are read from the text files in the
         'maps' folder beside the game when it starts.  The game will
         not start without them: if a map is missing or damaged, it
         names the file and the line at fault.  editing.doc explains
         how to change the maps.

     F-key usage
     ===========

         Mode        Fkey         Shifted      Ctrl   Alt+1..=
         ----------- ------------ ------------ ------ ------------
         FKeySpells  Spells       Save Weapons Macros Load Weapons
         FKeyWeapons Load Weapons Save Weapons Macros Spells
         FKeyGear    Load Gear    Save Gear    Macros Spells
         FKeyMacros  Macros       Save Weapons Spells Load Weapons

         The Alt column uses Alt with the number row: Alt+1 .. Alt+9,
         Alt+0, Alt+- and Alt+= stand for F1 .. F12.  Earlier versions
         used Alt with the F keys; Alt+F4 now closes the game (see
         "Keys").

         Weapons and gear use F1 .. F9 for gear slots 1 .. 9 and F10 for
         slot 0.  F11 and F12 have no gear slot and do nothing there.

     Status Indicators
     =================
       There are status inidcators in the upper left area of the screen
     just below the main stats.  These give you indications of states your
     character might be in:

         L - The player has light
         S - The player is shielded (protection effects from used items)
         P - The player is poisoned
         H - The player is hasted
         G - The player is under the effects of Giant Strength
         F - The player has resistance to fire
         C - The player has resistance to cold
         Z - The player has resistance to electricity
         A - The player has resistance to arcane
         V - The player is levitating
         M - The player has resistance to mind attacks
         E - The player is ethereal
         e - The player is ethereal, but the effect is about to run out

       With the Improved Status preference these letters are drawn in
     colour, or the icons themselves are drawn at half size in their place.
     Off, the letters are grey.  The Tab Menu's Status Effects describes
     each one you have (see Tab Menu).

     Macros
     ======
       Version 6.3 introduced the ability to use macros.  Whenever you have
     something that you do that requires the same keystrokes, you can
     create a macro for it. Macros can also be used for mixed use of the F
     keys, for example, you can assign some F keys to select weapons and
     others for casting spells.

       Macros at this time can only be defined within the .cfg files,
     either the global unicorn.cfg or a character .cfg.  Note that like
     other options, a character .cfg that defines macros will override
     macros assigned to the same keys defined in unicorn.cfg.  This allows
     you to define macros that can be used globally amongst all your
     characters, and others that are character specific.

       Here is a sample .cfg file from one of my paladins that shows how to
     create macros:

       FKeyMacros
       M1=[1
       M2=[2
       M3=[3
       M4=[4
       M5=mf$E
       M6=mp$E
       M9=ms$E
       M10=mc$E

       The FKeyMacros line specifies that I want the unshifted F keys to be
     assigned to the macros instead of spells.  Normally macros are
     executed with <CTRL><Fkey>.

       The other lines define my macros.  M1 through M4 give me single key
     access to 4 different weapon sets in my gear manager.  M5 gives me
     single key cast of Find Traps spell, M6 will cast Protection From
     Evil, M9 will cast Slow Poison, and M10 will cast Cure Light Wounds.

       Notice how the macro definition is just simply the keys you would
     normally press inside the game.  The only difference is the $E, when
     the macro expander sees the $E it actually replaces it with the
     <Enter> key, and $e will be replaced by the <Esc> key.  To specify
     arrows, just use the number pad number equivalent, for example left
     arrow would be 4, right arrow would be 6.

       Let me also explain about my spell casts.  When you use the <m>agic
     command, you only need to type enough letters to make the spell name
     unique, the rest of the name will come up automatically.  In the case
     of my paladin, he only knows those particular 4 spells and so only
     needs to type the first letter of each spell for it to be unique. 
     When my paladin learns Cure Serious Wounds, then my F10 macro will no
     longer work, since c is no longer capable of identifiying a complete
     spell. In which case I will have to change my F10 macro, if I want to
     keep it as Cure Light Wounds, then I would change the macro line to
     read:

       M10=mcure l$E

       Macros can be executed in towns, dungeons, or arena combats.  They
     cannot be used upland, as the F keys have special meaning upland if
     you possess a particular artifact.  Macros also cannot be triggerd in
     subscreens (inventory, character record, etc).  However, if you want a
     macro to do something with your inventory, you can put in your macro
     the ability to enter inventory and do something.  For example, you
     could create a macro like this:

       M8=uj$Ec$E

       If you keep an identification item in slot j (Belt Back), then this
     macro will allow you to identify the item in slot c (Left Shoulder),
     just by pressint <CTRL> <F8>, or just <F8> if you are using
     FKeyMacros.

       Another good use of macros is if you have a piece of gear that
     grants you a special action when worn, but you do not want that piece
     of gear worn all the time.  For example, on one of my characters I
     have a Robe of Eyes, but its protection value is not as good as my
     main cloak, but I would like to use the power of the Robe of Eyes when
     entering a new dungeon level.  I have this macro defined:

       M9=uqT84$E$EiqT84$E$e

       This macro equips the cloak that I have in the last slot of my pack,
     uses it and then swaps it back for my main cloak.  Now all I have to
     do is press <F9> and I have single key access to the power of the Robe
     of Eyes, while not having to move gear around manually to use it.

       So, macros can only be triggered from the main screen, but macros
     can go into subscreens to do things.

       There is also one more place that a macro can be executed, and that
     is during name entry on new character creation.  I have in my
     unicorn.cfg macros like this:

       M10=$Em22444444424444442226268884444444$E

       I can hit F10 after entering my new characters name (Before pressing
     <ENTER>!) and the macro will hit all the remaining keys to build me a
     male elf paladin with stats appropriate for a paladin.  I have such
     macros for all the base classes.  When I want to start a new character
     all I need to do is enter the name and press the F key for the class
     that I want to build.

     Item Merchants
     ==============
       There is a new method for navigating the inventories of most item
     merchants. The keys available for browsing through their inventories
     are:

         <8><Up Arrow> - Move the selection cursor up, will scroll if there
                         are more items above.
         <2><Dn Arrow> - Move the selection cursor down, will scroll if
                         there are more items below.
         <9><PgUp>     - Will scroll upwards one page worth of items.
         <3><PgDn>     - Will scroll downwards one page worth of items.
         <7><Home>     - Move to the first page of items.
         <1><End>      - Move to the last page of items.
         <I>           - Pull up the info card for the selected item.
         <Enter>       - Purchase selected item.

       The <a> through <r> keys will also instantly move the selection
     arrows to the row marked by the appropriate key.

       With the Improved Interface preference, which a new character
     starts with, the merchants' rows have no letters.  The same keys move
     the cursor, which runs from the last item round to the first, <ESC>
     leaves, and a scroll bar beside a long list shows how far down it is.
     The other menus that choose by number work the same way, with <8> and
     <2> to move, <9>, <3>, <7> and <1> to jump, <Enter> to choose and
     <ESC> to leave: the temple, stable, bank, house purchase, travel
     agency, inn, school, pawn shop and Mech-Merchant, the party screen
     (where <Enter> shows the member's statistics, and <D>, <C> and <O>
     ask for the member with the cursor), and a new character's sex, race
     and class.

       A new character's sex, race and class menus also take <R>, which
     makes that choice and every one after it at random, spends the stat
     points at random too, and shows the finished character sheet.  On
     the sheet that ends making a character, <Enter> or <ESC> starts the
     game and <R> starts over: from the name, or from the class after
     rebirth.

       With the preference an item merchant lists the goods down the left
     and shows the selected item's info card on the right, as <I> would:
     its name in the colour of its rarity, its kind and rarity, what it
     does and its value.  The card follows the cursor, so these merchants
     have no <I>.  The spell shops keep their wider rows, and their <I>.

       Questions that wanted <Y> or <N> become a short menu of what to do,
     such as "Sell" and "Keep", where <ESC> answers no: removing a
     curse, rebirth and One with Nature at the temple, selling and
     identifying at the pawn shop and the Mech-Merchant, a trip at the
     travel agency, a search at the library, joining the Cavaliers and
     training a party member.  The game mode after rebirth, the library's
     kind of search and a party member's orders are chosen the same way.

       Where the game asks you to type a word of power, a word for a relic,
     the hours of a nap or a travel destination's description, <ESC>
     backs out without doing anything.  Off, only <Enter> ends them.

       At the pawn shop and the Mech-Merchant, Sell from Pack lists the
     items in your pack with the merchant's offer for each, and Identify
     from Pack the ones you have not identified with what each costs,
     beside the selected item's card.  <Enter> sells or identifies the
     item.  Off, they offer the items one at a time, as before.

       The inventory lists what you carry with the cursor and shows the
     selected item's info card on the right.  <Enter> opens a short menu
     of what to do with it: choose it for the command that opened the
     inventory, put it in your pack, swap it with an item from your pack
     (or equip one into an empty slot), or swap it with the item on the
     ground when that fits the slot.  <P>, <T> and <X> still work too.

       The pack is one numbered list of its 32 slots, with the card beside
     it.  <Enter> offers to equip the item (or take or choose it, for what
     opened the pack), to pick it up into the air, put the item in the air
     down or swap the two, to move it to another slot, or to cancel.
     Moving asks for the slot with the cursor, and swaps the item with
     whatever is there.  <Space>, <X> and <I> still work too.

       A house and the portable hole list their slots the same way, in one
     numbered list in place of pages.  <Enter> offers to take the item out
     into your inventory, or to store an item from your inventory in an
     empty slot, then the pack's other actions.

       <S> sorts the pack, a house or the hole: by Name, Name Reverse,
     Type (the kind of item, as its card names it), Rarity (the rarest
     first), Value (the most valuable first) or Quantity (the most first,
     by the count in brackets after a name; an item without one counts
     as one).  The items gather at the top and the empty slots after
     them, and items you have not identified come after the rest for
     rarity, value and quantity.

       The preference also shows an item's name in the colour of its rarity
     wherever the game names it among other words, with the words around
     it in their own colour: in the message log, at the merchants, in the
     Tab Menu and on the crafting screen.  A spell's card gives its class,
     level and mana a line each, and scrolls stand in the middle of the
     screen.  Off, these show as the game first wrote them, with scrolls
     at the left of the screen.

     Tab Menu
     ========
       With the Improved Interface preference, <Tab> opens a menu of
     commands in the box where the messages show, and <Tab> or <ESC>
     closes it again without taking a turn.  <8> and <2> move the cursor,
     the list scrolls like the options screen, and <Enter> runs the
     command, which types the keys shown beside it.  At the top, in green,
     is everything around you: the merchant or house beside you, each
     creature beside you to talk to, attack or ask to join, each door to
     open, close or jam, and the items, chests, torches, lore, stairs and
     altars under you or a step away (a step away, the command steps there
     first).  Pick Up puts an item in your pack, as <G> does, or takes it
     up as <g> does when the pack is full or, without the Unified Pickup
     change, when only that counts it: a trading card joins your
     collection, and a dungeon's treasure on its last level completes
     the quest.  On the upland it offers the town or dungeon you stand on
     or beside, by its name, and your ship.  The common commands follow,
     then Save, Load, and Save and Exit.  A name too long for a line,
     such as a shop's, carries on over the next, and the cursor moves
     from command to command.  Off, <Tab> is not one of the game's
     commands, as before.

       In an arena fight it offers each monster beside you to attack and
     each item under you or a step away, though not one on the arena's
     edge, as the step there would end the fight first.  With the Arena
     Exit change, Leave Arena shows once every monster is dead.  Only the
     arena's own commands follow, without Save, Load, or Save and Exit,
     as a fight cannot be saved.

       Some commands show only when they can be used: Fire when your main
     hand holds a bow, wand or energy weapon, Throw for a dagger or
     throwing weapon in either hand, Heal when you are wounded, Transcribe
     a spell when you carry a spell scroll, and Say a Word of Power when
     a word may be said.

       Status Effects puts a ^ under the status indicators (see Status
     Indicators) and shows the one it points at in the box below: its
     name, what it does, and how long it lasts or where it comes from.
     <4> and <6> move to the next indicator you have, and <ESC> goes
     back to the menu.  With none, Status Effects is greyed out.

       Spells, in a town, dungeon or arena fight when you know a spell,
     asks for one to cast as <m> does.  With the Easy Spells preference
     it lists the spells you know instead, each with the mana it costs,
     and <Enter> casts the one under the cursor.  Spells you lack the
     mana for are grey and cannot be picked, and the cursor starts on
     the spell you cast last.  <ESC> goes back to the menu.

       Say a Word of Power, in a town or dungeon while your voice is
     rested, asks for a word as <w> does.  With the Easy Words preference
     it lists the words of power you have read of in lore, each with what
     the lore says it does, and <Enter> says the one under the cursor;
     Type a word asks for one instead.  <ESC> goes back to the menu.

       Save offers Overwrite, Save as New and Cancel.  Save as New asks
     for a name, and asks Yes or No before replacing a game already saved
     under it.  Load saves the same way, then lists the saved games, the
     newest first, and the one you pick starts at once.  For a character
     with permadeath both are greyed out.  Save and Exit saves and leaves
     the game, as <X> does.

     World Map
     =========
       On the upland, <M> shows the whole world: the twelve regions side
     by side, as large as the screen allows, with only the land you have
     seen.  Markers show where you are, your ship where you left it, and
     each town, dungeon, village and the Gateway you have found.  The
     direction keys move to the nearest marker that way, and the one
     chosen is named in yellow under it.  When you stand on a town or
     dungeon, your own marker takes its name.  <ESC>, <Enter> or <M> goes
     back.  The map takes no time, though without the Free Upland Keys
     change <M> passes a turn, as every upland key does.

       The World Map preference (O) draws the land in the small map's
     colours (Color) or with each tile's own art (Art), or turns the map
     off (Off), and then <M> does nothing.  It is kept in unicorn.cfg as
     WorldMap, so it applies to every character.  While the map is on,
     the Tab Menu offers it on the upland as World map.

     Persistant Dungeons
     ===================
       The RNG used for creating dungeons is now tied to a world seed that
     is generated when you create your character.  This means that the
     floor maps will remain consistent for your character anytime you
     revisit the same floor of a dungeon.  This persistance extends to the
     rooms, corridors, doors (secret and normal), traps, alters, and
     runestones.  This makes it possible for you to draw maps of your
     dungeons that you can then use to navigate dungeons in the future.  If
     a character goes through the ritual of rebirth then a new world seed
     is generated and therefore a whole new set of dungeon maps.

       In addition to the persistant maps, dungeon contents (items laying
     on the floor and living mobs) will remain persistant until the dungeon
     is reset. So if you left an item and some mobs on level 1, leave the
     level and come back, you will find level 1 exactly how you left it,
     the items will still be there and the same mobs will be there.  Mobs
     are now spanwed only when the dungeon is reset, so you can even leave
     the dungeon, go to town, and come back and the dungeon will be just
     how you left it, mobs, items, explored territory will all be as you
     left it.

       A dungeon is reset under two condtions, either entering it for the
     first time or if you visited a different dungeon prior to reentering a
     dungeon. so you can leave, come back, leave, come back, all you want
     and the dungeon will stay how you left it so long as you do not enter
     another dungeon, at which point a reset will occur.

       When a dungeon resets all items and mobs currently in the dungeon
     will be removed and all levels of the dungeon will spawn new random
     mobs.  You will also forget any explored territory in a reset dungeon
     and must explore it again.  Of course if you drew maps of the dungeon
     floors, you should have no difficulty finding your way around.

     Gear Manager
     ============
       A simple gear manager has been added to the game that allows you to
     save up to 10 gear configurations.  This allows you to easily change
     out your gear depending on the situation.  For example you could put
     your main battle gear in slot 1, your ranged gear in slot 2, your
     anti-magic monster gear into slot 3, MLV gear into slot 4, etc.  The
     gear manager allows you to swap just your weapons or all your equipped
     gear.  The slots saved by the gear manager are: Main Hand, Off Hand,
     Neck 1, Neck 2 All 4 ring slots, Armor, Shield, Helm, Cloak, and
     boots.

       The following new commands have been added with the gear manager and
     may be used in dungeons, arena combat mode, and upland:

       { - Save just your main and off hands to a specified gear slot
       [ - Recall just your main and off hands from a specified gear slot
       } - Save all equipment to a specified gear slot
       ] - Recall all equipment from a specified gear slot

       When using any of these commands you will receive a prompt for which
     gear slot is to be used, 0 through 9.

       With the Improved Interface preference, <G> in the inventory shows
     the ten gear slots beside your equipment.  The slot under the cursor
     shows what it keeps in place of your equipment, an item you no
     longer carry in grey, and <Enter> equips the slot, or just its
     weapons, or saves your gear, or just your weapons, into it.  <G> or
     <ESC> goes back to the inventory.

     Charged Throwables
     ==================
       Charged throwables have now been added to the game.  So far there
     are only two kinds, bags of rocks or throwing stars.  These items are
     used with the <t>hrow command and must be held in either your main or
     off hand.  If held in your off hand you may also equip a single-handed
     weapon in your main hand.  When <t>hrowing a charged throwable one
     charge will be used each time you throw.  Once all the charges are
     used up then the item will disappear from inventory.

     Auto Heal Item Selection
     ========================
       Now when you use the <h>eal command, if your healing skill is on
     cooldown a healing item will be found in your inventory and used
     instead.  Healing items are bandages, healing potions, scrolls, etc. 
     The first item found will be the item used.  The search for a healing
     item starts with main inventory and searches from top to bottom.  If
     no healing item is found in your main inventory then your pack will be
     searched, again from top to bottom.  You can influence which items
     will be used for healing by the order you place them in your
     inventory.  You can of course still use the <u>se command if you want
     to heal with a specific item.

     Auto Eat Item Selection
     =======================
       The <e>at command will no longer ask you to select an item to eat. 
     The <e>at command now searches your inventory for a food item and
     consumes it automatically.  The inventory search works like it does
     for healing items, so if you want to influence which item the auto-eat
     used, place it earlier in your inventory.  If you wish to eat a
     specific item then you can still <u>se a food item from inventory. 
     the <e>at command works this way both upland and in dungeons.

     Auto Selection
     ==============
       The commands: <o>pen doors, <c>lose doors, <t>alk and <j>oin will
     now no longer ask you for a direction if only 1 choice is possible. 
     If only a single choice is possible the action will be carried out
     without further input from the user.  If more than one possibility
     exists for these commands then the game will ask for a direction.

     Ranged Targeting
     ================
       There is a targeting system for when attacking ranged targets.  When
     you select an action that requires a ranged target you will get a
     message in the message area to "Select Target" and you will get a red
     * symbol on your character.  Use the normal movement keys to move the
     targeting * to the point you wish to target the ranged action.  Once
     you have the targeting * on the target press <enter> to perform the
     action.  Ranged actions come in two forms, ones that will stop at the
     targeted spot and ones that will keep going until they hit something. 
     <t>hrow and <f>ire are both targeted actions, whatever you are
     throwing/firing will stop where you specified with the targeting *. 
     Most magic spells and ranged staves will continue on past the target
     until they hit a monster or other obstacle.  In all cases if the
     projectile hits something before reaching the target spot, it will
     stop and possibly affect what it hit.

     Dual Wielding
     =============
       All classes are capable of dual wielding once some basic
     requirements are met.  First the character must have a minimum skill
     level of 25 in Combat, then the character must have a minimum skill
     level of 25 in the weapon types being dual equipped.  For example, to
     equip a sword and a hammer the player will need a Combat skill of 25+,
     an Edge skill of 25+ and a Blunt skill of 25+.

       The weapon equipped in the offhand position will only do 50% of its
     normal damage.  While dual wielding weapons your shield will not
     contribute to your armor rating.  Any enchantments or socket effect on
     your shield will still work.

     Effects of base stats
     =====================
         Strength     - Increases melee damage
         Dexterity    - Increases chance to hit/chance to dodge
         Intelligence - Increases chance to hit with magic
         Wisdom       - Increases mana regeneration
         Constitution - Increases health regeneration
         Charisma     - Improves haggling with vendors

     Mutable Weapons/Armor
     =====================
       There is a class of weapons/armor that can take on numerous
     attributes. These items will vary in strength and number of
     enchantments based upon the level of the item.  These items can be
     found from the very start of the game, however they are quite rare,
     but most will be more powerful than other items found at similar
     levels.  Mutable items can usually be identified because once
     identified the item name often has a prefix and up to two postfixes.
     Mutable items are not fixed in the database but are rather generated
     on the fly when they are found.

     Legacy Weapons/Armor
     ====================
       Legacy weapons/armor are another class of special items.  Legacy
     gear actually levels along with you.  Initially these items may seem
     rather weak but as you gain levels, so does the item.  As items level
     they become stronger and start acquireing enchantments.  The names of
     legacy items do not stand out from their base item, but can be
     identified because they are very rare items with common names.  Like
     Mutable items, legacy items are not fixed within the database but can
     take on many different configurations of enchantments.  Unlike Mutable
     items which have their power set at the level they were found, legacy
     items get stronger as the wielder gets stronger.

     Legendary Gear
     ==============
       Legendary gear is the most powerful gear that can be found in the
     game.  Legendary gear is extremely rare, and with the Improved Items
     preference its name shows brown in your inventory once identified,
     as every unique item's does.  Legendary gear is normally named after
     the NPCs that first appear in the Legends score table.  Legendary
     gear always has 3 enchants on it, typically of use to the class that
     the original owner was.  For example, if you find Nina's item the
     enchants are most benificial to the wizard class, while Elan's item
     is most useful to a warrior.  Legendary items also always come with
     3 open sockets.

     Magic Spells
     ============
       A function key system for spells, like the one in "The Land" also
     exits in Quest.  This system allows you to save your 11 most common
     spells into F2 .. F12 for easy access.  F1 always contains the last
     spell cast. To program a function key, press 'm' for magic, then
     type the name of the spell, when the full spell name appears, press
     the F-key you want to store the spell into, then press <ESC>, or
     <ENTER> to cast it as well.  Now all you have to do to cast that
     spell is press the F-key you assigned the spell to, then <ENTER>.

       Spell name entry has been made as easy to swallow as possible: start
     to type the name of your spell, and as soon as the computer can see
     your letters as the start of only one spell you know, it shows the
     rest of the name in grey.  If this is the spell you wish to cast,
     press <ENTER>, or <Tab> to fill in the name, otherwise keep typing.
     A spell whose whole name you type is cast even when a longer name
     begins with it, as Heal is beside Healing.  <?> lists the spells you
     know, and <ESC> casts nothing.

       The spell "Sticks to Snakes" works only in the combat mode and not
     in the standard dungeon mode. so dont cast this spell unless you are
     in the combat mode.

     Words of Power
     ==============
       Lore tells of words of power, said with <w> in towns and dungeons.
     Once said, a word rests your voice for a while, whether it works or
     not; a word that is no word of power costs nothing, nor does <ESC>
     with the Improved Interface preference.  With the Easy Words
     preference the Tab menu lists the words you have read of, a word you
     type is filled in once its first letters fit only one of them, and
     the message log says when a word fails or backfires, when what you
     said is no word of power, and how long your voice still needs.  A
     relic that answers to words lists and fills in its own the same way.

     Sockets
     =======
       Weapons, armor, rings, and necklaces may now be found with sockets. 
     Each socket will appear on the name line as () if it is empty or else
     between the parenthesis will be the effect generated from whatever is
     in the socket.

       To socket a gem or jewel into a socketed item, <U>se the gem or
     jewel and then select the item in the inventory screen that you want
     to socket with the gem.  An item can have a maximum of 3 sockets, once
     all the sockets on an item are full, you cannot add any more
     socketable items.

     Socket Effects
     ==============
         STR+   - Increases strength
         DEX+   - Increases dexterity
         INT+   - Increases intelligence
         WIS+   - Increases wisdom
         CON+   - Increases constitution
         CHR+   - Increases charisma
         Mp+    - Increases mana
         Hp+    - Increases health
         AP+    - Increases attack power
         DEF+   - Increases armour protection
         Hit+   - Incrases chance to hit
         HpR+   - Increases health regeneration rate
         MnR+   - Increases mana regeneration rate
         All+   - Increases all base stats
         Exp+   - Increases experience gained
         MF+    - Increases chance to find rarer items
         Hp/Mp+ - Increases both health and mana points
         MGR+   - Increases magic resistance
         MLV+   - Increases monster level
         CRT+   - Increases chance for critical damage
         DDG+   - Increases dodge chance
         SPP+   - Increases spell power

     Item Colors
     ===========
         Unidentified         Cyan, and yellow on the floor; with
                              Improved Items, the colour of its
                              rarity, after a *
         Common               Gray
         Uncommon             White
         Rare                 Green
         Very Rare            Blue
         Ultra Rare           Purple
         Unique               Brown, once identified
         Legendary            Light red, once identified, or red if
                              it is unique too
         Legacy               Yellow, once identified
         Special              Yellow: an item never found as loot,
                              such as shop goods and crafted items
         Quest Item           Cyan: a dungeon's treasure or another
                              piece of a quest, whatever its rarity

       These are the rarities an item's card names, though it names a
     unique or legendary item Unique, Legendary or Unique Legendary
     instead, and legacy gear Legacy.  Unique, Legendary, Legacy and
     Quest Item need the Improved Items preference.  With it Off, names
     follow older colour bands: some Very Rare items show purple, the
     very rarest show yellow, and uniques, legendary and legacy gear and
     quest items show the colour of their rarity.

     Auto-Identify
     =============
       When an item is picked up for the first time there is a chance that
     you will automatically identify the item.  The rarer the item is the
     less likely you will be able to auto identify it.  Increased Lore
     skill will increase the chances for auto identification.  With the
     Improved Items preference a common item is identified as soon as a
     monster drops it.

         Rarity         Base Chance     Increase per point of Lore
         Common         100.000%        
         Uncommon       25.000%         5.000%
         Rare           2.500%          1.000%
         Very Rare      0.250%          0.150%
         Ultra Rare     0.025%          0.020%

     Rebirth
     =======
       Rebirth allows you to begin the game again with your character. 
     During the rebirth process you will be able to select a new class and
     select whether you want to play in normal, heroic, or epic mode.  Your
     character will start again at level 1 with level 1 stats.

       Why would you want to do rebirth?  Simply put you get to start over
     with all your current inventory!  So if you found some powerful gear
     you can start the game over already possessing that gear.  This allows
     you to complete the game quicker and therefore place higher on the
     Hall of Legends score table.  In addition to this every rebirth adds
     to your magic find stat making it easier to find that better gear!

       During rebirth you will not lose any trophies you have already
     collected, but any progress towards trophies you have not yet acquired
     will be reset and you will have to begin your journey towards those
     trophies again.

       Any quest rewards or items will be removed from your inventory
     during rebirth, so if you have any quest items belonging to object
     quests, you may want to consider not performing a rebirth until after
     completing the object quest. There is an exception to this, one of the
     object quests requires a large number of components, these components
     are not considered quest objects and will not be removed.  It should
     be fairly obvious which items these are. Objects acquired as the
     result of an object quest are not considered quest objects and will
     not be removed by rebirth.

       In order to perform the Ritual of Rebirth, visit any druidic temple.
     If you qualify for the ritual it will appear as one of the options. 
     In order to qualify you must have completed the main goal of the game
     and not be playing in casual mode.

     Notes to get you started
     ========================
     - When generating your character:  Your initial stats start at 10
       each, you are then given a point fund of 18 to distribute amoung
       your 6 base stats. use the arrow keys to distribute this point fund,
       when the number to the right of the arrow is zero, press enter to
       save you selections.
     - Go into the town where you begin (Radon) and talk to the people,
       they will give you clues as to where to find dungeons, or
       information about the realm in general.
     - Travel in the wilderness takes a lot of time and therefore a lot of
       food. Be sure you have plenty of food before travelling far in the
       wilderness.
     - Some terrain types in the wilderness take more time to travel
       through, so it is best to stick to roads when possible.
     - If you do run low on food in the wilderness, get off the road and
       <s>earch, you may just find some food.
     - The first, easiest, and closest dungeon can be found directly north
       of Radon at the base of the mountains.
     - in dungeons, use the <s>earch command often, there are many hidden
       traps and doors throughout the dungeon levels.
     - Always make sure to carry your weapon in your MAIN hand. 
     - If you are getting low on Hit Points, find a good safe place and
       take a <n>ap, this will gain your Hit Points back at a faster rate
     - Seasonal items are based upon the real calendar month and not the
       in-game month.
     - The <Enter> key is used to back out of sub-screens. 
     - You gain a damage bonus when using weapons your class has an
       affinity for. The starting weapon skills will indicate which weapon
       your class uses, when using weapons of this type you will obtain the
       bonus.
     - Keep an eye out for unidentified weapons and armor.  Most basic
       weapons are common and therefore identified when picked up.  However
       you can find mutable and legacy gear as early as level 1.  If a
       vendor is willing to pay you more than what seems normal for an
       unidentified piece of gear then it is worth identifying it first, it
       just might be mutable, legacy or a legendary, which are typically
       more powerful than gear normally found at the same level.
     - Legacy gear levels when you do, so it is worth holding on to even if
       it is not more powerful than what you currently have equipped, in
       time it will be better.  With the Improved Items preference legacy
       gear is easy to spot in your inventory: a simple name colored
       yellow.  Remember the item must be identified first before the
       color will be yellow.

     Note about Score Tables
     =======================
       There are 4 different high score tables maintained by the game. 
         Legends                 This table contains the highest level
                                 attained by class.  This table is
                                 pre-filled with a selection of NPCs.
         High Scores             This table holds the highest level
                                 attained by class.  This table starts
                                 empty.
         Hall of Legends         This table holds those heroes who have
                                 actually completed the main goal of the
                                 game.
         Hall of Remembrance     This table holds those heroes who have
                                 fallen.

